<title>USA: U.S. Q1 GDP rev to +5.9 pct from +5.8 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. Q1 GDP rev to +5.9 pct from +5.8 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-06-27</dateline>
<text>
<p>Commerce seasonally adjusted GDP data on a chain-weighted basis. Following are annualized percent changes from prior quarters, in 1992 chain dollars.</p>
<p> -				     Q1-F---Q1-P-----Q4---1996</p>
<p> Gross Domestic Product	   5.9    5.8    3.8    2.4</p>
<p> Final Sales of Domest.Product  4.1    3.8    4.9    2.7</p>
<p> Final Sales to Domestic Buyers 5.2    5.3    2.6    2.8</p>
<p> GDP Price Index		    2.7    2.8    1.9    2.1</p>
<p> Implicit Price Deflator	  2.2    2.2    1.5    2.0</p>
<p> Consumer Spending		  5.6    5.7    3.4    2.5</p>
<p> -Durable Goods		    18.8   19.3    5.0    5.4</p>
<p> Pct Change,'92 chain dollars:    Q1-F---Q1-P-----Q4---1996</p>
<p> Consumer Spending-Nondurables  4.6    4.9    1.8    1.4</p>
<p> Consumer Spending-Services     3.4    3.3    3.8    2.4</p>
<p> Business Investment	     11.0   11.5    5.5    7.4</p>
<p> Housing Investment		 6.7    6.0   -1.8    5.3</p>
<p> Exports of Goods/Services     10.8   11.2   25.0    6.5</p>
<p> Imports of Goods/Services     19.9   23.2    3.3    6.4</p>
<p> Govt.Purchases/Investment	0.1    0.1   -0.9    0.8</p>
<p> -Federal			    -3.2   -3.1   -5.3   -1.1</p>
<p> -State and Local		   2.1    2.0    1.9    2.0</p>
<p>A-Advance. P-Preliminary. F-Final.  </p>
<p> Blns of '92 chain dollars:	 Q1-F-----Q1-P-------Q4-----1996</p>
<p> Business Inventory Change     48.6     51.4     17.1     13.6</p>
<p> -NonFarm			    49.2     52.0     19.3     17.1</p>
<p> -Farm				 -1.1     -1.2     -2.6     -4.0</p>
<p> Net Exports Goods/Svcs	-120.7   -126.8    -98.4   -113.6</p>
<p> -Exports			   885.3    886.0    862.9    825.9</p>
<p> -Imports			 1,006.0  1,012.9    961.3    939.5</p>
<p> Government Purchases	 1,273.8  1,273.6  1,273.4  1,270.6</p>
<p> -Federal			   459.2    459.3    462.9    467.1</p>
<p> -State and Local		 815.7    815.3    811.4    804.3</p>
<p>A-Advance. P-Preliminary. F-Final.  </p>
<p>Wall Street economists had expected the final estimate of first quarter GDP to show a 5.9 percent rise and the price index to be up 2.8 percent. A forecast for the implicit was unavailable.</p>
<p>Commerce provides three estimates of GDP per quarter. The first is the advance (A) estimate, the second is the preliminary (P) estimate, and the last is the final (F) estimate.</p>
<p>The 5.9 percent jump in the first quarter GDP was the biggest since fourth quarter 1987's rise of 6.0 percent.  </p>
<p>The 2.7 percent gain in the first quarter GDP price index was the biggest rise since the first quarter of 1995 when it advanced at a rate of 3.3 percent.</p>
<p>The 5.6 percent gain in consumer spending in the first quarter was the biggest since the first quarter of 1992 when spending was up 6.4 percent.</p>
